  "worked_solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $M(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  ${R}^{2}$ to vectors in ${R}^{3}$ (not in ${R}^{2}$).\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $P(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  $R$ \n(not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
